\details{
With an additive shift, the ADE is \eqn{E[\partial \mu(A, X) / \partial a]}, where \eqn{\mu(a, x)} is the expected outcome given treatment \eqn{a} and covariates \eqn{x}: the slope of the outcome with respect to the treatment for each unit at the treatment it actually received, averaged over units. It is the change in the mean outcome per unit of an infinitesimal shift of every unit's treatment (Hines, Diaz-Ordaz, & Vansteelandt, 2026). Because it involves only treatment values near those actually received, it requires positivity only locally, whereas the ADRF and its derivative, the AMEF, require that every unit could have received each treatment value in the range.

The ADE differs from the AMEF averaged over the treatment. The AMEF at a treatment value \eqn{a} averages the slope at \eqn{a} over the distribution of the covariates in the whole sample, whereas the ADE averages each unit's slope at its own treatment value. The two coincide when the treatment is unrelated to the covariates (e.g., in a randomized trial) but differ otherwise. A single-number summary of the ADRF itself is the slope of its linear projection; see \code{\link[=curve_projection]{curve_projection()}}.

With \code{shift_fun = "multiplicative"} in \code{\link[=shift_curve]{shift_curve()}}, the ADE is \eqn{E[A \, \partial \mu(A, X) / \partial a]}, the change in the mean outcome per unit proportional increase in every unit's treatment; multiplying it by .01 gives the change for a 1\% increase. More generally, for a function \eqn{g(a, \delta)} supplied to \code{shift_fun}, the ADE averages each unit's slope weighted by the derivative of \eqn{g} with respect to \eqn{\delta} at 0. \code{ade()} can only be used when \eqn{g(a, 0) = a} for every unit, i.e., when a shift of 0 leaves every unit's treatment unchanged, and when the range of shifts in \code{x} includes 0.

The ADE is computed as the derivative of the interpolated shift curve at 0, using the same method as \code{\link[=amef]{amef()}}, and its standard error is computed from that of the shift curve using the delta method, or from the bootstrap replicates or posterior draws of the shift curve when those are used. For smooth outcome models, it agrees closely with the ADE computed directly from the predictions (e.g., by \code{\link[marginaleffects:avg_slopes]{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()}}) and does not depend on the range of shifts. For outcome models whose predictions are step functions of the treatment (e.g., Bayesian additive regression trees), the derivative of each unit's prediction is 0 almost everywhere, and the ADE computed from the shift curve reflects the change in the mean outcome over the spacing of the grid of shifts, which depends on \code{shift} and \code{n} in the call to \code{\link[=shift_curve]{shift_curve()}}.

